Subject: Emit an unnamed namespace's members in the namespace enclosing it

An unnamed namespace is defined to behave as a namespace whose name nothing can
write, followed by a using-directive for it, so its members are found by lookup
in the namespace which encloses them. `namespace { struct A {}; }` at the top of
a header declares a type whose only C++ spelling, from any translation unit
which includes that header, is `A`.

With `--enable-cxx-namespaces` that namespace currently gets a module of its
own, named `_bindgen_mod_<id>` because there is no name to use. Neither half of
that name is usable. `_bindgen_mod_<id>` is not a C++ name, so a consumer which
writes C++ from the output - a wrapper generator, a test harness which names the
types back - emits a namespace which does not exist; and `<id>` is
`Item::exposed_id`, a counter over every item parsed, so the Rust path moves
whenever anything earlier in the header is edited. An allowlist cannot reach
into it either: `--allowlist-type A` is matched against
`root::_bindgen_mod_12::A`, and the `12` is not something a build file can
write down.

An inline namespace has the same transparency and is already handled this way:
`<Module as CodeGenerator>::codegen` emits no module for one, `Item::compute_path`
and `Item::real_canonical_name` leave it out of the paths and names they build,
and `Item::codegen_depth` does not count it. The result is output which names the
enclosing namespace, which is valid, because that is where lookup finds the
members. This extends each of those to a namespace with no name, which needs it
more: an inline namespace's own name is at least a name C++ would accept.

`conservative_inline_namespaces` has no counterpart here, and cannot have one. It
exists so that a caller who wants an inline namespace's own name in the path can
ask for it; an unnamed namespace has no name to ask for.

# Where the enclosing namespace declares the same name

Unlike an inline namespace, an unnamed one can declare a name the enclosing
namespace also declares, the two being different scopes as any two namespaces
are:

    namespace { constexpr int LIMIT = 1; }
    constexpr int LIMIT = 2;
    static_assert(::LIMIT == 2);

These are two entities, which an inline namespace could not give you: a member
of one is a member of the enclosing namespace for redeclaration too, so the
second `LIMIT` would be a redefinition. Qualified lookup resolves `::LIMIT` to
the enclosing declaration rather than finding both, because a using-directive is
consulted only where direct lookup found nothing - so C++ can name one of them
and not the other. Rust has one namespace for both, and emitting both into one module would
be a redefinition; emitting one and dropping the other silently would answer with
the wrong constant.

So `compute_transparent_unnamed_namespaces` decides, once, which unnamed
namespaces are transparent: all of them, except one whose members would land
under a name already taken. Such a namespace keeps its module, under the invented
name, because that module is the only thing left which tells the two apart - and
a consumer which meets the invented name knows it cannot spell that item in C++,
which is the truth. The decision is taken deepest-first, so that an unnamed
namespace inside an unnamed namespace is judged against the first named scope
above them both, which is where both sets of names would land.

Which pairs of names count is not the same question for each kind, so
`Declarations` keeps them apart. A variable or a function is only ever filed
there by a declaration that is really present, and two of them under one name is
either a redefinition or - worse, because the second is dropped - the wrong value
quietly standing in for the right one. A module is the same, and a module sharing
a name with a type is a redefinition too, Rust having one namespace for both.

Two *types* are allowed to meet, and have to be. A type is parented into
whichever module was being traversed when it was first referred to, so a
namespace appears to declare types it merely mentions - `uint32_t`, or a type
declared outside it and named by one of its members - and refusing on those would
refuse nearly every unnamed namespace there is. A consumer which is handed two
types under one name can see that it has, which is not true of the variable that
silently went missing.

The pass runs as codegen is entered and before anything else, because
`resolve_typerefs` and `process_replacements` ask items for names and for
allowlisting paths, and an allowlisting path is memoized - a decision taken after
them would reach some items and not others.
