+    ///
+    /// An unnamed namespace behaves as a namespace whose name nothing can
+    /// write, followed by a using-directive for it, so lookup in the enclosing
+    /// namespace finds its members - and there is no name to give a module of
+    /// its own anyway. That is how an inline namespace is already treated, and
+    /// the reason applies with more force here.
+    ///
